        <mxCell id="u51uzEtMC7baKAof4C2t-77" value="&lt;h1 style=&quot;margin-top: 0px;&quot;&gt;Conclusion&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;- To optimize the logic of the process, we can skip any prior starting point which have been processed, by declare an array to store any squares will be processed and then check the next square whether exists in the array.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- The feature of the assignment can be extened to to find many possible shortest paths, to achieve that, we need to define a flag or mark for each of square to demonstrate as a current level of its, because we want to limit to proccess only squares that has the same level and the destination square in it, from that only the same shortest paths will be included in result. At the point where we firstly found the shortest path, we should include this path to an array, then continue to process other squares have the same level, then if we found the next shortest path, we can push to the array.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- The time complexity of finding path of Knight Moves will be O(n2) and space complexixty will be O(n2)&lt;/p&gt;" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;overflow=hidden;rounded=0;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="350" y="910" width="540" height="310" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
